00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.390
Before we dive into
BigQuery machine learning,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.960
let's talk a little bit
about what BigQuery is.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.460
So BigQuery is our Cloud
data analytics warehouse.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.010
It lets you store and query lots
and lots of data super fast.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.810
What do we mean by lots of data?

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.340
Petabyte-scale
storage and queries.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.635
And you can do it
all in familiar SQL.

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:26.010
The great thing about BigQuery
is that it's fully managed

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:26.720
and serverless.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:28.553
So you don't have to
worry about spinning up

00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:31.000
any of the infrastructure
to run complex queries.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.690
BigQuery will handle
all of that for you.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.760
So BigQuery is a
great starting point

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.160
for machine learning because
you often need a lot of data

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.702
to get started with
machine learning.

00:01:39.702 --> 00:01:42.712
But if you haven't ever built a
machine learning model before,

00:01:42.712 --> 00:01:43.920
that process can be daunting.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.425
There's a lot to learn.

00:01:45.425 --> 00:01:46.800
It's also expensive
for companies

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.440
to hire enough data scientists
to build these machine learning

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.430
models.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.370
And it's complex and time
consuming to take your data out

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.650
of a data warehouse, like
BigQuery, and feed it

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.297
into your machine
learning pipeline.

00:01:58.297 --> 00:01:59.880
So let's talk about
the process of how

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.220
we would build a custom machine
learning model without BQML.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.040
We would first
identify a problem

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.082
that we would want to
solve with machine learning

00:02:08.082 --> 00:02:09.107
and gather our data.

00:02:09.107 --> 00:02:10.690
Then we'd preprocess
our data, so we'd

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.790
want to get it into the right
format for our machine learning

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:13.290
models.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:15.460
So this means if we
had categorical data,

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.370
we would need to one-hot
encode that into arrays.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.938
Then we would split our data.

00:02:19.938 --> 00:02:21.730
So an important concept
in machine learning

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:23.120
is train/test split.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.240
So you take about
80% of your data, use

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.640
that to train your model,
and you set aside about 20%

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.760
for testing to see how
your model performs on data

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.343
that it's never seen before.

00:02:32.343 --> 00:02:33.760
Then in step four,
you're actually

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.302
writing the code to
build your model,

00:02:35.302 --> 00:02:37.090
choosing your
model architecture,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.830
and training and
evaluating your model.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.110
You might repeat steps four
and five a couple of times

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.960
until you get accuracy
that you're happy with.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.540
Then finally, you'll need
to spin up infrastructure

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.240
to deploy your model so that
you can handle prediction

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.310
requests from your users.

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:55.220
The great thing about BQML is
that it simplifies this a lot.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.450
So with BQML, all you need
to do is identify a problem

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.370
that you want to solve
with machine learning,

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:03.010
gather your data,
write a query to train

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.690
and evaluate your model.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:09.090
And then it's automatically
available to get predictions.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.710
So just to summarize some
really great features of BQML,

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:14.490
it lets you execute ML
models without moving

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:15.570
your data from BigQuery.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.150
So you don't have to take your
data out of the data warehouse

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:19.567
and feed it into
your ML pipeline.

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:21.750
You can do it all
right from BigQuery.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.610
You can iterate on
your models and SQL.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.980
So it's really fast
to train new models,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.520
try out different sets
of features and labels.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.870
And BQML also
automates common tasks

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.330
like hyperparameter tuning
and train/test split,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.530
which I talked about before.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.530
So this is all of
the SQL code you need

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.625
to build and train your model.

00:03:40.625 --> 00:03:42.250
In this example,
we're building a model

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:44.380
to predict how many
bikes are in circulation

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.810
in New York given some data
on the weather for that day.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.140
So we tell it the type
of model we want to use,

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.410
the labels that we're
using for the model.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.670
And then we tell it what
table to grab the data from.

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:56.470
And once this query
finishes, we've

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.060
got a trained model ready to go.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.730
Similarly, to
generate predictions,

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.720
you just call ML.PREDICT.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.960
And when you run
this, BQML will add

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.390
a column called predicted
underscore and then whatever

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:12.320
your label name column name was.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.420
So there's some really
awesome new and upcoming

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.029
features in BQML that
I want to highlight.

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:19.240
Model evaluation charts are
now available in the UI.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.740
So once your model trains, you
get a little bit more insight

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.750
into how that
training process went

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:26.000
and what evaluation looks like.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.228
K-means clustering
models are in Beta.

00:04:28.228 --> 00:04:30.020
And then we have a
bunch of different Alpha

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.982
features here-- matrix
factorization, the ability

00:04:32.982 --> 00:04:34.940
to import TensorFlow
models that you've already

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.010
trained externally
and query them

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:42.870
in BQML, feature preprocessing
functions, and TensorFlow DNN

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:46.200
Classifier and DNN
Regressor models.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.840
So if you want to
try any of these out,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.920
talk to your sales reps.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.660
Or reach out to me,
and I can connect you

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:51.493
to the right people.

00:04:51.493 --> 00:04:55.657
Just send me a DM on
Twitter @SRobTweets.

00:04:55.657 --> 00:04:57.740
So I never like to get too
far into a presentation

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.995
without showing some demos.

00:04:58.995 --> 00:05:00.620
So we're going to
see some demos of two

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.490
different types of BQML models.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.310
The first will be
a regression model.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:06.200
Regression model just
means that our model is

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.882
predicting a numerical value.

00:05:07.882 --> 00:05:09.590
So we're going to see,
given the weather,

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:12.630
how many New York City
Citibikes are in circulation.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.390
And then for our
classification model,

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.610
I've got some data on
different types of candy bars

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:18.188
because everyone loves candy.

00:05:18.188 --> 00:05:20.480
And we're going to see, given
some data on a candy bar,

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.200
is it chocolate or not?

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.210
So let's go to the demo.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.920
So this is the BigQuery UI.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.750
Make it a little bit bigger.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:31.020
There we go.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.700
I've got some
saved queries here.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.460
So the first query I'm going
to run is a prediction query,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.640
and this is on my bike model.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.410
And so what this
is going to do is

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.120
it's going to query
my bike model.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.790
And I've got a test table here
with, I believe, just four

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:52.660
examples of data that wasn't
included in our training set.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.210
And we'll see if our model
can get close to predicting

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:56.723
the actual number of trips.

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:59.140
So this is what the query looks
like, very similar to what

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:00.670
you saw on the slide.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.360
And I'm going to run that.

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.800
And it's done, and we can now
compare predicted num trips

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.158
with actual number of trips.

00:06:07.158 --> 00:06:08.950
And we can see that
it's pretty close here.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:13.390
So we get 20,000,
20,000, 12,000, 12,000.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:16.300
All those predictions
are looking pretty good.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.760
So that was a prediction
for a regression model.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.420
Now I'm going to show a
prediction for our chocolate

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.860
classification model.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.630
So let's take a
look at the data.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.290
Here's our test data.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.500
And what this model
is going to predict

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.480
is whether or not
it's chocolate.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:38.390
So 0 for not chocolate,
1 for chocolate.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:39.890
And so when we run
this query, we'll

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:42.182
see that the output is a
little bit different than what

00:06:42.182 --> 00:06:44.580
we get in the regression model.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.960
So this is telling us the
predicted class for each input.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.510
But we also get data
on the probability

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.670
that the particular input
belongs to this class.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:58.130
So what this is telling us
is that there is a 97% chance

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:01.550
that this is a
chocolate candy bar

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:03.830
and then 3% chance
that it's not.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:07.340
Similarly for this
one, we get 93%.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.980
And then these two, it predicted
both of these correctly

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.430
as chocolate.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.070
So we get 99% confidence
for this one and nearly 98%

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:18.240
confidence for that one.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.440
So that is how you generate
predictions on models in BQML.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.390
But I also want to
show you how easy it is

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.085
to train a new model in BQML.

00:07:29.085 --> 00:07:30.710
So I want to show
you the query that we

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:34.880
use to train that chocolate
classification model.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.010
And I'm actually going to
change this a little bit.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:39.385
So this was a
classification problem.

00:07:39.385 --> 00:07:41.510
We're going to change this
to a regression problem.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:44.120
So another item
from our data set

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:49.430
is the popularity of each
candy bar as a percentage.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:51.720
In the data set, it's
called winpercent.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:52.970
So we're going to change this.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.910
And all we need to do to
change it to a regression model

00:07:55.910 --> 00:08:00.560
is change this model type
to linear regression.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.150
We now need to
collect our new label

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:04.070
column, which is winpercent.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:08.680
And then we will change
the labels to that.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.470
So I'm going to kick this off.

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.580
It should take about
a minute to run.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.530
But again, the great thing
about what BQML is doing here

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.940
is it's making this whole
process a lot simpler.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.790
So it used to take
weeks or maybe even

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:24.990
months of doing all these
steps of collecting your data,

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:26.490
getting it into
the right format,

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:29.850
converting categorical
data to arrays.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:33.678
Under the hood BQML is taking
care of all that for you.

00:08:33.678 --> 00:08:35.220
So when our model
finishes, we should

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.150
be able to run a query very
similar to the bike regression

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.309
query that we saw here.

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:45.360
And you saw how easy it is to
develop models iteratively.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.180
So if I wanted to
add new features,

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.423
maybe switch my label,
it's just a matter

00:08:50.423 --> 00:08:51.840
of changing a
couple lines of SQL.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:56.170
I don't need to rebuild my
model code from scratch.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.630
So BQML really simplifying
a lot of these steps for us.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.820
And again, the query should
finish in about a minute.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:04.360
There we go.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.282
So our model's created.

00:09:06.282 --> 00:09:07.740
We will go to our
model, and we can

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.360
see that this is what our
model schema looks like.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.750
So we'll get this
predicted winpercent value.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:17.190
And if we go back to
our Saved queries,

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.300
we will just modify
this query slightly

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.410
to query from our new
model, which we called

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:26.130
chocolate_percent.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:30.090
And we'll add the
winpercentage field to it, too.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:33.510
So we'll run this query
and see how our model does

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.740
at predicting candy popularity.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:38.670
So here we get the
predicted percentage of 42.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.970
If we scroll over here,
the actual was 39.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.990
We can compare the
rest of them, 39, 35.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:49.680
And it looks pretty close across
the board for our predicted

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.840
and our actual.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.400
So there you can
see how simple it

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:58.143
is to generate predictions and
also train new models in BQML.

00:09:58.143 --> 00:09:59.685
So if we could go
back to the slides.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.370
So there's an endless
number of possibilities

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.245
of different model types
that you could build

00:10:09.245 --> 00:10:10.490
in BigQuery machine learning.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.863
I'm not going to go into
all of these on this slide,

00:10:12.863 --> 00:10:14.780
but you get the idea of
how you could apply it

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.932
across many
different industries.

00:10:17.932 --> 00:10:20.390
And the other product that AAA
used in their implementation

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.670
is Apps Script and
what is Apps Script?

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:25.220
Apps Script is a serverless
JavaScript runtime

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:26.390
for G Suite automation.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:27.950
So it lets you
really easily connect

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:29.488
different Google products.

00:10:29.488 --> 00:10:30.530
It's entirely serverless.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:33.540
So the Code Editor
lives in your browser,

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:36.050
but the scripts run
on Google servers.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.935
It's written in a language
based on JavaScript.

00:10:38.935 --> 00:10:40.310
So if you know
JavaScript, you're

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.700
good to go getting
started with Apps Script.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.710
And it lets you automate
tasks across different Google

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:46.923
products.

00:10:46.923 --> 00:10:48.590
I really like this
code snippet example.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:50.540
So what this is doing
is in the first row

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.060
we're getting a row from
a spreadsheet, which

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.600
is an address.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.230
Then we're creating a
static map of that address

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.030
and then emailing
that map to somebody.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.470
So in just five
lines of code, we

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.520
connect Google Sheets,
Google Maps, and Gmail.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:06.518
And you can also
connect additional APIs

00:11:06.518 --> 00:11:08.810
using Apps Script through
what's called advanced Google

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:09.920
services.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.800
So this is what we'll use to
connect BigQuery in the demo

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:14.460
from Matt that
you'll see later on.

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:16.150
So using the advanced
services, you

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:18.150
don't have to worry at
all about authentication.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.630
So it's just as if you were
using the regular BigQuery

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:24.180
client library in
your Apps Script code.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.560
G Suite Add-ons allow
developers to build

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:30.620
many applications that live
alongside top G Suite apps.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.840
They're installable domain-wide.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:34.600
And we use Add-ons in
the demo that you'll

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.030
see shortly to embed
a tool within Google

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:37.885
Sheets as a sidebar.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.590
So just to summarize
the different use cases

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.840
that we've seen
with BQML so far,

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.750
we've seen customers use BQML
to simplify existing workflows.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.170
So take existing machine
learning pipelines,

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:55.410
custom pipelines, and then
move those over to be BQML.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.660
We've also seen BQML
as a great entry point

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:58.842
to machine learning.

00:11:58.842 --> 00:12:00.300
So for data analysts
that might not

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.538
have experience with
ML and their data

00:12:02.538 --> 00:12:04.080
is already in
BigQuery, they can just

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.510
write some SQL queries to
get started training custom

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.028
machine learning models.

00:12:08.028 --> 00:12:10.320
And then we've also seen it
used to complement existing

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:11.845
machine learning workflows.

00:12:11.845 --> 00:12:14.220
So let's say you have a custom
machine learning pipeline,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.743
but you've got a new idea.

00:12:15.743 --> 00:12:17.160
And you want to
test it out to see

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:19.118
if it'll work, to see if
there's a relationship

00:12:19.118 --> 00:12:23.030
between a specific set
of features and labels.

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:26.730
So we've seen a bunch of
customers using BQML so far.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:28.370
And I'm really
excited to introduce

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.880
Dr. Chuck Brooks, the director
of data strategy at AAA's Auto

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.610
Club Group, to tell us
more about their use case.

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:37.230
Take it away, Chuck.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:40.530
CHUCK BROOKS: Thank you, Sara.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:44.550
I am Dr. Chuck Brooks, and I
am from the Auto Club Group.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.790
How many folks know of AAA?

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:48.360
Oh, yeah.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.670
How many AAA members do we have?

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:51.480
Even better.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.170
That's what I'm talking about.

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:55.830
OK.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.850
So for you that know
about us, you probably

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:00.560
know about our
roadside services.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:01.580
Certainly, if
you're a member, you

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:02.955
know about our
roadside services.

00:13:02.955 --> 00:13:07.010
It's pretty common
service that we offer.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:10.010
But perhaps you didn't
know that we really

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:12.800
have 12 lines of business.

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:16.790
And many of them have
nothing to do with roadside.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:20.120
So we offer a full
travel agency.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:26.150
Perhaps you didn't know that we
have a full bank, that we offer

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:29.640
life insurance and full
line of insurance products,

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:30.690
including home and auto.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:38.660
In many of the states that we
service, we do car repairs.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:43.530
So automotive repair
services as well.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.060
What do these all
have in common?

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.030
Well, for us, it's all
about the customer.

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:49.340
It's all about offering
services to our customer

00:13:49.340 --> 00:13:52.730
that enrich their experience
and their lives and the things

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:54.150
that they have to do every day.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.568
It's all about handling
those tough situations.

00:13:57.568 --> 00:14:00.110
I mean, when your car is broken
down on the side of the road,

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.610
or you get up in the morning
and you go out to start the car

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.957
and it doesn't start,
you need some help.

00:14:05.957 --> 00:14:07.290
And we want to be there for you.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:17.800
So when our customers
call, it's important

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:20.380
that we be there to
answer the phone.

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:23.560
Of course, we're doing digital
transformation like many folks.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:27.580
And you can do a lot more on
your phone, or with a text,

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.350
or on our mobile app.

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:33.160
But when you reach out, we
need to be there for you.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:45.110
And so when I joined Triple AAA
just some short 10 months ago,

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:49.350
we needed to bring
the data together

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:51.180
from all of those
lines of business.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:57.440
Like many companies, our
data was siloed inside

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.660
of each one of the
lines of business.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:02.863
And in fact, the different
lines of business and the data

00:15:02.863 --> 00:15:04.280
there couldn't
talk to each other.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:11.380
Have you experienced that
before in your organizations?

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.150
Would you be surprised
if I told you

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:16.180
that the banking customers,
the banking organization

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:18.610
didn't know who our 10
million members were?

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:25.910
I was, and I've seen this.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.130
I've been doing this
for almost 40 years.

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:30.950
And I've seen silos of
data, but we had taken it

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:31.840
to a whole new level.

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.020
So we began to
build a data lake.

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:38.810
And we began to use GCP products
to do that, primarily BigQuery,

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:40.760
but the rest of the
data stores as well.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:42.200
And we began to
move our data in,

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:43.675
and we began to integrate it.

00:15:43.675 --> 00:15:45.050
And we began to
put it in formats

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:48.790
where it was usable where
analysts could look at it.

00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:50.380
And they could
understand what was

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.291
happening across organization.

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:02.590
But as we did that, one of
the things that I noticed

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:07.210
was that we hadn't made
it easy for analysts

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:11.080
who weren't SQL developers to
necessarily get to that data.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:17.880
And so I looked across
the GCP platform.

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:19.350
And I said, well,
what can we use?

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.660
What tools can we
bring into play

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:24.780
that allow analysts who
not aren't necessarily

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:32.050
SQL developers to be more
effective in our environment?

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:38.570
And I thought, BigQuery, Google
Sheets, because everybody

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:39.740
knows spreadsheets.

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:43.560
Analysts love spreadsheets.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:46.410
And BQML.

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:48.330
And I said, how can we
bring these together

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:55.380
to begin to predict how many
call center agents we need

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:57.960
to have available
at any given time

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:00.420
so that when our customers
call, we can be there?

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:07.030
And so I reached out to one
of our trusted partners.

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:09.819
I hope you guys know the
folks from Maven Wave.

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:11.920
We work with them regularly.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:14.270
They do some great work with us.

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:15.849
They don't try to
do the work for us.

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:19.160
They like to work with us.

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:20.660
And if you're working
with partners,

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:23.480
you know how important that is.

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:24.827
So let me introduce Matt.

00:17:24.827 --> 00:17:27.160
And Matt can show you a little
bit about the application

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:28.069
that we've created.

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:31.440
It's been sanitized a little
bit, but you'll get the idea.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.380
MATT POWERS: Oh, thank
you, Dr. Chuck Brooks.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:35.420
That was a great intro.

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.780
So yeah, Maven Wave is a
Google Premier Partner.

00:17:37.780 --> 00:17:39.250
On the right-hand
side here, we've

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:42.320
got the different ways that
Maven Wave's broken down

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:43.553
to its different practices.

00:17:43.553 --> 00:17:45.220
And it's actually
pretty closely aligned

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:48.280
with the different Google
products that are out there.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.200
So Data Analytics,
something I'll

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:53.020
be focusing on today,
things like BigQuery ML

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:56.500
and all the other great machine
learning tools and capabilities

00:17:56.500 --> 00:18:00.130
that Google has, and everything
from enterprise collaboration.

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:03.920
So helping companies integrate
G Suite into their environments.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:07.698
So again, thank you,
Dr. Chuck Brooks.

00:18:07.698 --> 00:18:08.990
So now focusing on the problem.

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:13.590
So as Chuck mentioned,
being able to answer

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:16.830
the call at the different call
centers is crucial to AAA.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:18.590
Being there when
someone calls when

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:22.730
they're in an accident
or their vehicle--

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:24.540
a flat tire, whatever
it may be, they

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.100
need to be there
to answer the call.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:30.490
So that was really
crucial for this use case.

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:33.600
So we've got some of the KPIs
for AAA call centers, things

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:35.850
like the customer
experience, the agent

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:37.380
utilization, the cost.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:40.020
And all of these things
can be tied back to,

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:41.640
is an agent in the call center?

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:44.400
Do they have the right
staff for this call center?

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:46.523
So with that in
mind, the problem

00:18:46.523 --> 00:18:47.940
that we're trying
to solve is, how

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.315
can we always make sure that
someone is there in the call

00:18:50.315 --> 00:18:54.290
center to answer the call?

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:56.540
So with that being the
problem, what was the solution?

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:58.290
And Chuck and Sara
have both hit on this.

00:18:58.290 --> 00:19:00.170
But we wanted to
make it very easily

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:01.910
to run a lot of
different scenarios,

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:05.250
to be able to predict what that
call demand was going to be.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:09.020
So we built a model using
BQML, as Sara walked through.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:11.120
We then wanted to expose
the model to users

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:14.210
throughout the organization
in a very easy to use way.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:16.720
And we did that through Google
Sheets and the Google Sheets

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.477
Add-on.

00:19:18.477 --> 00:19:20.810
And again, by doing this,
it's allowed users with little

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:23.600
to no ML experience
to really utilize

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.110
the power of machine
learning to run

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:28.343
these different
what-if scenarios.

00:19:28.343 --> 00:19:30.260
So the end result before
we jump into the demo

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:33.590
here, so we created
a BQML model.

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:35.390
We integrated that
into Google Sheets.

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:39.890
And then that integration was
possible through Apps Script.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.770
Other last note here is it was
two weeks of development time.

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:45.705
So these tools, because we're
using both GCP and G Suite,

00:19:45.705 --> 00:19:47.080
the integration
between these two

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:50.500
was fairly simple
and pretty quick

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:56.128
development time to get to a
good minimum viable product.

00:19:56.128 --> 00:19:57.920
So with that, I'd like
to turn over a demo.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:01.610
Great.

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:02.810
So what have we here?

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:04.058
We've got a Google Sheet.

00:20:04.058 --> 00:20:05.600
Most people are
going to be familiar.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:06.975
Most users at AAA
are going to be

00:20:06.975 --> 00:20:08.880
familiar with this
type of experience.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:12.110
You don't need any SQL skills to
navigate your way around here.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:14.187
So we've got a few
different inputs

00:20:14.187 --> 00:20:15.270
that went into our models.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:17.840
So we've got things
like state, county,

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.820
month, what's the
members segment,

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:21.950
because AAA doesn't
want to just predict

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:23.540
the call volume of a county.

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:26.010
They want to predict the call
volume of a specific member

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:27.080
segment.

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:28.730
We've also got things
like population

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:31.440
and the per capita
income during that year.

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:34.080
And then finally the member
count in that community

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:35.955
and then that member segment.

00:20:35.955 --> 00:20:38.580
So what we've done here is we've
created two what-if scenarios.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.160
So we've said,
what if per capita

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.490
goes up for the
Florida Bay County?

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.060
And then the second
one we looked at,

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:46.610
what if the member
count increases

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:48.770
by 10% every single year?

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:51.260
How is that going to change
the call volume for that call

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:51.920
center?

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:57.680
And then from that, what
sort of hiring decisions

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:01.010
does AAA need to make in
order to staff that properly?

00:21:03.707 --> 00:21:05.290
So we've got or two
what-if scenarios.

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:08.320
And what I've done up here is
just, as Sara was mentioning,

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:10.360
this is a Google Sheets Add-on.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:14.710
So again, this was created all
through Google Apps Script.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:16.372
What I'll do is
I'll highlight all

00:21:16.372 --> 00:21:18.580
of the things that go into
the models, so the xrange.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:24.970
And then I'll tell BQML where
I want the output to display.

00:21:29.326 --> 00:21:31.115
So I hit run model there.

00:21:31.115 --> 00:21:32.490
And what it did
behind the scenes

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:34.800
is we created a model
in BQML, and then we're

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:36.060
now surfacing the results.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:39.330
So we gave it the input
data, and now we're

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.880
surfacing the results
in column I here.

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.030
So we can see that for the first
scenario, as per capita income

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.760
goes up, it looks like
there, the predicted call

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:51.065
volume will go up slightly.

00:21:51.065 --> 00:21:52.440
The next one, if
the member count

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:54.420
increases by 10%
every year, there

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:57.870
will be some substantial
growth in the predicted call

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:01.080
volume for AAA.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:02.910
So that at a high
level is the demo.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:05.752
And it's just highlighting all
the different what-if scenarios

00:22:05.752 --> 00:22:06.960
that an analyst can now play.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:09.210
They can change the
state, change this county,

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:10.650
change a member segment.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:12.810
What happens if the
population decreases?

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:16.170
So a bunch of what-if
scenarios that can

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:19.297
be utilized by anyone in AAA.

00:22:19.297 --> 00:22:20.755
So now, I'm jumping
back to slides.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:26.480
So a few more.

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.375
Diving a little bit deeper into
what problem does this solve,

00:22:29.375 --> 00:22:30.500
and how does this help AAA?

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:35.440
So machine learning is becoming
easier and easier to use.

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:37.860
Now that you've opened
up to spreadsheets,

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:40.580
the users that have the ability
to interact with spreadsheet

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:43.540
is obviously greater than the
users that have SQL skills

00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:46.540
or even specific machine
learning Python skills.

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:49.090
So greater adoption
is definitely possible

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:53.520
using something like this
through Google Sheet.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:58.140
Now talking about the
model delivery cycle.

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:00.840
So here's the
typical ML process.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:02.880
You start with a
business objective.

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:05.170
And then all the things
highlighted in blue

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:10.830
are areas where BQML can really
help you with the full model

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:12.090
lifecycle process.

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:14.760
The first two there,
the exploring and data

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:16.860
selection and then
the preprocessing,

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:18.740
most of time in machine
learning work, that's

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:22.020
70% of the challenge and 70%
of where the time's spent,

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:25.260
is getting the right amount
of data, getting enough of it,

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:28.920
making sure you do that
exploratory data analysis,

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:30.960
you find what features
are important,

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.670
and then the preprocessing.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:35.060
So BQML can't solve
all these problems,

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:38.230
but it does do a few key things
to help you with this process.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:39.810
So the explore and
data selection.

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:44.115
So in the example I just
showed, per capita income

00:23:44.115 --> 00:23:46.950
and population, I was
actually able to get that

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:48.900
through BigQuery's
public data sets.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:51.720
They've curated great
public data sets, everything

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:54.190
from census data
to weather data,

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:55.770
all sorts of great information.

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:59.360
And the ability to
join that open source

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.470
data set into your model
is just a SQL join.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.720
You don't need to go out and
buy any sort of data set.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:05.140
It's just a SQL join.

00:24:05.140 --> 00:24:06.750
So that was the first thing.

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:08.340
As far as the
exploration, it was all

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:11.885
done through a SQL interface,
which is fairly easy.

00:24:11.885 --> 00:24:13.260
And then they've
also got a bunch

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:15.960
of other great tools, things
like Dataprep and Data Studio,

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:19.440
to do that data visualization
and that exploratory data

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:23.300
analysis which is so important
for machine learning problems.

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:24.570
Then the preprocessing.

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.287
So some great things
that it handles

00:24:26.287 --> 00:24:27.370
behind the scenes for you.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:28.560
So one-hot encoding.

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:31.450
So for those of you who are
familiar with machine learning,

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:33.870
if you have any categorical
features like the state

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:36.510
or the county, then
you need to convert

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:39.810
that into some array, as
Sara was talking about,

00:24:39.810 --> 00:24:42.900
so essentially
rows of 0s and 1s.

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:45.647
That's all done for you
automatically behind the scenes

00:24:45.647 --> 00:24:46.230
with BigQuery.

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:49.215
So it can handle
categorical features.

00:24:49.215 --> 00:24:50.590
And then it handles
missing data.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:53.880
So if you have any
missing data, there's

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.800
ways that you can set how
you want it to handle it.

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:59.940
But out of the box, it'll
put the median value in there

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:00.840
for you.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:04.800
So it won't solve all the
preprocessing and data

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:07.560
selection steps, but there's
a lot of great things

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:10.680
that make that process easier
in the Google ecosystem

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:12.840
and with BQML.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:16.080
And then the rest of the cycle,
BQML can handle a lot of this.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.060
So model selection Sara hit on.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:21.168
They're coming out with more
and more models every day.

00:25:21.168 --> 00:25:23.460
And then the train, evaluate,
tune, predict, and serve,

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.240
that is all done through BQML.

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:29.523
So now we've created this model.

00:25:29.523 --> 00:25:30.940
We've exposed it
in Google Sheets.

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:33.743
How do you actually deploy
this in an organization?

00:25:33.743 --> 00:25:35.660
And that's what this
slide is getting at here.

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:38.320
So we've got our data
scientists and data analysts

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:40.390
building these models in BQML.

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:44.140
They've built model A, B, and
C for predicting call volume,

00:25:44.140 --> 00:25:45.960
predicting where
accidents are going

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:48.760
to happen, all these different
things that you can predict.

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:51.522
Now I want to expose
this to the organization.

00:25:51.522 --> 00:25:53.230
So we have the Google
Sheet on the right.

00:25:53.230 --> 00:25:54.688
And really, the
only thing you need

00:25:54.688 --> 00:25:58.300
to do to expose that model
to your organization is you

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:01.150
need to give a user who
wants to use a model

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:03.580
access to the underlying
data set and the model

00:26:03.580 --> 00:26:04.780
inside of BigQuery.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:07.540
So it's as simple as
provisioning a user access

00:26:07.540 --> 00:26:10.210
to that model and that data set.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:14.080
And then users throughout the
organization can utilize this.

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:18.670
The last step in the areas that
we know as AAA to the Maven

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:21.520
Wave partnership we
need to grow this

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:24.640
is giving users better
insights into how

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:25.678
the model's performing.

00:26:25.678 --> 00:26:27.220
So we're just giving
them the output.

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:28.240
But what's the accuracy?

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:30.610
Give users the
confidence interval

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:35.260
that the predicted call volume
is going to be between x and y.

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:37.450
So instead of just giving
them a black box here's

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:40.330
the answer, giving
them more insight into,

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:42.730
how accurate is this?

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:44.080
So it's not just used blindly.

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.540
So that's an area that we're
working on and figuring out

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:48.770
the best way to
expose that to users

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:54.235
who, again, aren't as familiar
with BigQuery machine learning.

00:26:54.235 --> 00:26:55.860
So with that, I'm
going to turn it over

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:57.870
to Chuck to talk about
some of the value

00:26:57.870 --> 00:26:59.822
they've seen so far with AAA.

00:26:59.822 --> 00:27:02.670
CHUCK BROOKS: Thanks, Matt.

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:05.940
So was that cool?

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:07.770
Is that cool stuff?

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:11.100
I mean, these guys do a
great job of presenting that.

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:12.780
That's great stuff, right?

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:15.450
Let me ask you,
do you think folks

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:17.850
in your organization,
analysts in your organization,

00:27:17.850 --> 00:27:18.720
can use these tools?

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:22.130
Probably?

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:23.060
Yeah?

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:24.560
OK, good.

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:28.550
Look, we've got 3,000 agents
across five call centers

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:30.960
handling over 15
million calls a year.

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:35.190
And I got to tell you that
this is working for us.

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:37.320
Early MVP results
show that we can

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:41.640
begin to reduce call times
because we're accurately

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:46.620
able to put the right staff
in place on a regular basis.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.672
Now, that's huge for us
for all of the reasons

00:27:48.672 --> 00:27:49.380
that I mentioned.

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.540
When our customers call,
we need to be there.

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:54.890
